Fillets: the guide past an open end of the spine follows the curve the end
edge is a piece of, where that goes on past the edge, instead of a smooth
turn into the edge's tangent (mitcad#415).

The case: the root of a thread (the helical edge between its two flanks,
a piece of one helix or of an iso-line of a flank's B-spline surface) cut
by the faces at its ends, e.g. a nut's thread running from below the nut
to above it, its roots rounded up to the nut's top and bottom. The walk of
the blend starts and ends some way past the spine's ends, along the guide
(ChFi3d_PerformElSpine) extended there by GeomLib::ExtendCurveToPoint
towards a point twenty times as far along the end tangent. On a helix of
small lead that extension leaves the root's helix within millimetres,
the sections of the walk past the end turned away from the flanks, the
contacts swerved, and the faces at the ends were cut along curves joined by
vertices with tolerances of tens of millimetres (the result invalid, or
valid with such vertices, which a later boolean merged with far away
vertices of other faces).

- ChFi3d_OwnCurveExtension: the guide extended past its open end along
  the end edge's own curve (a non-periodic B-spline going on past the
  edge), by up to the length the extension needs, reparametrized with the
  guide's speed at its end so that the guide's own part keeps its
  parameters (checked), the two joined (GeomConvert_CompCurveToBSplineCurve)
  where they meet within tolerance and tangentially.
- ChFi3d_IsoCurveOfEdge: the iso-line of a B-spline surface of the end
  edge along which the edge runs (its curve on that face a line along one
  parameter), oriented as the spine runs: the curve the edge is a piece of
  where the edge's own curve stops at the edge's end.
- ChFi3d_PerformElSpine: at an open start or end of the spine, the guide
  is extended along the edge's own curve, else along that iso-line, where
  one goes on past the end by more than the extension needs (cut to the
  extension's length there); else the smooth extension continues from as
  far as one goes (as before where neither does).

Before, the result was invalid, its vertices up to tens of millimetres
off. Test: a ring's bore threaded from below the ring to above it, the
thread's root rounded up to the ring's faces (geometry test_sweeps): valid,
the vertices within 1e-3, the volume added that of the section the ball
fills turned over the ring's height, and each face's gain that section
turned through the angle the root rises across it.

